id: decisions/035-enforcement-locus
title: "Shape lives in the schema, relations in the runtime, and both must be able to fail"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'enforcement-locus'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: enforcement-locus
axis_gloss: "where a constraint actually lives and gets checked: declared in the schema/contract, or asserted by a script or test at run time"
poles:
  a: schema-declared-constraint
  b: runtime-check
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: full
leverage: 7.2
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(6) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Where does a rule live so that it can fail — in the schema as a const or
  required key, in a script or test at run time, or both — and may a schema
  exist in schemas/ that no test ever compiles?

why_it_matters: >
  A const in a schema no test compiles is a rule that cannot fail, and the
  repository's own standard is that a rule that cannot fail is not a rule; today
  the projection-preference schema is exactly that while its runtime resolver
  carries 441 test lines, and elsewhere the ranking policy and the
  no-self-authorization rail are locked as schema consts. Once answered, every
  contract a bot ships below Dave must say which of its rules the schema holds
  and which the runtime holds, and a test must be able to make each one fail.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    3 judgments sit at schema-declared-constraint (0 authored) and 3 at
    runtime-check (1 authored). The repository asserts both directions on this
    axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave regards the @yawn/contracts package schemas under contracts/ as
    inside this rule or as a separate surface (schemas/node.yawn calls them
    parallel).

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Schema-declared. Every constraint that JSON Schema can express — consts,
      enums, required, additionalProperties — is declared there and nowhere else;
      runtime code handles only what schema cannot (referential integrity, order,
      cycles, privacy); a runtime check that duplicates a schema rule is removed.
    cost: "Move roughly twenty projection-preference error codes into schema where expressible; delete duplicated runtime enum checks in lib/; a test compiling every schema."
    risk: "Rules that are only partly expressible get split across two layers with the schema half looking complete; the assertSchemaAndRuntimeReject belt-and-braces in orientation-map is dismantled."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "tests/schema-coverage.test.mjs: every schemas/*.schema.json is compiled by at least one test (projection-preference fails today); a lint that lib/*.mjs does not re-check an enum its schema owns."
    evidence_for:
      - "J1092"
      - "J1104"
      - "J1116"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Runtime-check. The resolver or reducer is the authority; a schema
      documents shape and may be absent or uncompiled; a constraint counts as
      enforced when a test exercises the runtime path. schemas/node.yawn says schema
      validity is advisory.
    cost: "One line in schemas/node.yawn; nothing else changes."
    risk: "Schema consts such as selfAuthorizationAllowed: false and notAuthority: true become documentation, and a runtime that forgets to check them passes; the 'canonicalState const false' pin that keeps the orientation draft out of canon is no longer load-bearing."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "None new; note in schemas/node.yawn that schemas are advisory."
    evidence_for:
      - "J1118"
      - "J1209"
      - "J1230"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Both, with the division written on every contract, as the
      agency-holarchy $comment already writes it for one. Shape, enums, consts and
      required keys live in the schema and every schema in schemas/ MUST be compiled
      by a test. Relational rules — referential integrity, authorization, event
      order, acyclicity, privacy, timestamps — live in the runtime and MUST be named
      as deferred in the schema's $comment. Where a rule can live in both, the test
      asserts that both reject (the assertSchemaAndRuntimeReject pattern in
      tests/orientation-map-v0.1.test.mjs) so neither layer can drift silently. A
      schema no test compiles is a defect, not a draft.
    cost: "One coverage test; a $comment on each schema naming what it defers; extend assertSchemaAndRuntimeReject to projection-preference; no rule moves."
    risk: "Dual assertion doubles the test surface for every shared rule and the exception list of runtime-only rules grows without review."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "tests/schema-coverage.test.mjs: every schemas/*.schema.json is imported or compiled in tests/ or scripts/, and every schema carries a $comment naming what it defers to runtime; tests/projection-preference-v1.test.mjs compiles schemas/projection-preference.v1.schema.json and asserts both layers reject the same malformed preference."
    evidence_for:
      - "J1118"
      - "J1209"
      - "J1092"
      - "J1104"
      - "J1116"
      - "J1230"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.8
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    Three judgments on each side and only one authored — but that one (J1118, the
    agency-holarchy $comment) already states C's division: JSON Schema checks
    shape; reducers and policy engines must also check integrity, authorization,
    order, acyclicity and privacy at runtime. The passing suite implements C's
    dual-rejection pattern for orientation-map (J1209, with its explicit exception
    list of relational rules), and the three schema-declared consts on side A
    (ranking policy, the no-self-authorization rail, canonicalState false) are
    exactly the kind of rule C says belongs in the schema: rails that must not be
    configurable. So side A and side B are not in conflict; they are the two
    halves of a division one file wrote down. The defect that makes this an axis
    at all is J1230: schemas/projection-preference.v1.schema.json exists and no
    test compiles it, so whatever it declares is decorative. C names that as a
    defect and makes it fail. Genuinely split by count, not by structure.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "A test in tests/ fails if any schemas/*.schema.json is compiled by no test or script, and passes — which requires tests/projection-preference-v1.test.mjs to compile its schema."
    - "Every schema in schemas/ carries a $comment naming the rules it defers to runtime, and a test checks the comment is present."
    - "The projection-preference test asserts schema and runtime both reject at least one shared malformed case, on the orientation-map pattern."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A schema in schemas/ changes an enum or const and no test fails; or a runtime
    error code exists with neither a schema rule nor a $comment naming it as
    runtime-only.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J1092"
  - "J1104"
  - "J1116"
  - "J1118"
  - "J1209"
  - "J1230"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
